{"@context":{"lean":"lean:","block":"block:","imports":{"@type":"@id"}},"@graph":[{"@id":"lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RigidTensorDual","@type":"LeanModule","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.RigidTensorDual","imports":["lean:module:Init","lean:module:Mathlib.CategoryTheory.Monoidal.Rigid.Basic","lean:module:QOU.MathlibExt.RightDualIsoUnique"],"declarations":8,"chunks":1},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualIso_tensorExactPairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualIso_tensorExactPairing","kind":"theorem","line":134,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RigidTensorDual.lean#L134-L167","doc":"**The dual comparison of a tensor product is the tensor of the dual comparisons.**\n\nBoth `tensorExactPairing X X₁ Y Y₁` and `tensorExactPairing X X₂ Y Y₂` exhibit a right dual of\n`X ⊗ Y`, so `rightDualIso` compares them; the claim is that this comparison factors as the tensor\nof the two componentwise comparisons.  Note the **order reversal** on the right: the dual of a\ntensor is the tensor of the duals *in the opposite order*, so the `Y`-comparison comes first.\n\nProved by uniqueness (`rightDualIso_hom_unique`): it suffices that the right-hand side satisfies\nthe same evaluation identity, and that is the two componentwise identities plus one application of\nthe interchange law.  Purely formal — no braiding, no rigidity beyond the two pairings. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tensorDoubleDualIso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tensorDoubleDualIso","kind":"def","line":125,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RigidTensorDual.lean#L125-L132","doc":"**The double dual of a tensor product**, `(X ⊗ Y)ᘁᘁ ≅ Xᘁᘁ ⊗ Yᘁᘁ`.\n\n`tensorRightDualIso` twice.  The order reverses on each application, so it comes back to `X, Y`\nrather than staying swapped — which is exactly why the *double* dual, and not the single one, is\nwhat a **monoidal** pivotal structure compares against. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualMapIso_inv","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualMapIso_inv","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RigidTensorDual.lean#L117-L117","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualMapIso_hom","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualMapIso_hom","kind":"theorem","line":117,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RigidTensorDual.lean#L117-L117","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualMapIso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.rightDualMapIso","kind":"def","line":116,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RigidTensorDual.lean#L116-L123","doc":"Right-dualising an isomorphism.  Contravariant, so the mate of `i.inv` is the forward map. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tensorRightDualIso","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tensorRightDualIso","kind":"def","line":107,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RigidTensorDual.lean#L107-L114","doc":"**Duals reverse order**: `(X ⊗ Y)ᘁ ≅ Yᘁ ⊗ Xᘁ`, in any right-rigid category.\n\nBoth are right duals of `X ⊗ Y` — the canonical one, and the one `tensorExactPairing` builds —\nso `rightDualIso` compares them. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.hasRightDualTensor","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.hasRightDualTensor","kind":"def","line":101,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RigidTensorDual.lean#L101-L105","doc":"In a right-rigid category, `X ⊗ Y` has `Yᘁ ⊗ Xᘁ` as a right dual. ","signatureChunk":0},{"@id":"lean:QOU.MathlibExt.tensorExactPairing","@type":"LeanDeclaration","name":"QOU.MathlibExt.tensorExactPairing","kind":"def","line":58,"sourceLink":"https://github.com/litlfred/qou/blob/7341d18daa5d103cc9fa41bebdf054eda96ad3d7/folio/quantum-observable-universe/lean/QOU/MathlibExt/RigidTensorDual.lean#L58-L99","doc":"**`X ⊗ Y` and `Y' ⊗ X'` form an exact pairing** — Mathlib's \"Future work\" entry 1.\n\nThe coevaluation creates the `X`-pair and nests the `Y`-pair inside it; the evaluation contracts\nthe inner `X`-pair first and then the outer `Y`-pair.  Duals reverse order, which is why the\nright-hand object is `Y' ⊗ X'` and not `X' ⊗ Y'`. ","signatureChunk":0}]}